* the Newtonian formulation of classical mechanics is great, by its simplicity and intuitiveness; but it is too difficult to use in complex situations [complex constraints]
* derivation from the Newtonian formulation
	* we want to work with energy, instead of forces in complex situations; hence our aim is to find a formulation of classical mechanics which is equivalent to the Newtonian and uses energies instead of forces
	* the Newtonian formulation is the law (f = m * a)
	* suppose x is the from time to position function
	* f = - gradient P
		* but we are interested only along x
			* fx = slope (along x) (-P)
	* a, v, K depending on time
		* m * a = slope (m * v)
		* =
			* we need to have v^2 to get kinetic energy
		* slope (slope (along v) (m * v^2 / 2))
		* =
		* slope (slope (along v) K)
	* hence
		* slope (along x) (-P) = slope (slope (along v) K)
	* note that
		* K depends on velicity but not on distance, hence
			* slope (along x) K = 0
		* P does not depend on velocity, hence
			* slope (along v) P = 0
	* hence we can just add to each side's energy the energy of the other side
		* slope (along x) (K + -P) = slope (slope (along v) (K - P))
	* with L := K - P
		* slope (along x) L = slope (slope (along v) L)
	* L is the "Lagrangian"
	* this is a variant of the mathematical Euler differential equation on
		* L (t, d, v) = m * v^2 / 2 - U d
		* d depending on t
		* v being the slope of d depending on t
	* hence the name "Euler-Lagrange equation" of this physical variant
